##### **1.1 3D Kinematic Vectors: Position, Displacement, Velocity, and Acceleration**

In three dimensions, the location of a particle relative to a chosen reference origin is specified by its **position vector** $\vec{r}(t)$. Decomposed into Cartesian unit vectors $\hat{\imath}$, $\hat{\jmath}$, and $\hat{k}$:

$$\vec{r}(t) = x(t)\hat{\imath} + y(t)\hat{\jmath} + z(t)\hat{k}$$

For a static particle located at coordinates $(-3\text{ m}, 2\text{ m}, 5\text{ m})$, the position vector is $\vec{r} = (-3\text{ m})\hat{\imath} + (2\text{ m})\hat{\jmath} + (5\text{ m})\hat{k}$.

When the particle travels from an initial position $\vec{r}_1 = \vec{r}(t_1)$ to a subsequent position $\vec{r}_2 = \vec{r}(t_2)$ across the time interval $\Delta t = t_2 - t_1$, its **displacement** $\Delta\vec{r}$ is the straight directed line segment connecting the two points:

$$\Delta\vec{r} = \vec{r}_2 - \vec{r}_1 = \Delta x\,\hat{\imath} + \Delta y\,\hat{\jmath} + \Delta z\,\hat{k}$$

where $\Delta x = x_2 - x_1$, $\Delta y = y_2 - y_1$, and $\Delta z = z_2 - z_1$. Displacement depends exclusively on the endpoint coordinates and is independent of the actual path traversed between them.

